IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc15-general.l4 -- sections 468 to 475 of the Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46 (Part XIV, Jurisdiction, "General"):
-- which court may try an indictable offence (ss 468, 469), jurisdiction over the person (s 470), trial by jury (s 471),
-- trial without a jury (s 473), the clerk's power to adjourn (s 474) and the accused who absconds during the trial (s 475).
-- Layer 1: the law, objectively, from the text alone; it takes nobody's point of view. Imports the core only (`ccc-core`).
-- Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30).
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
--
-- HOW A RESULT READS (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING (ccc15)"). Every decision answers a MAYBE BOOLEAN:
--   JUST TRUE   the facts bring the case within the provision, on the text's own words;
--   JUST FALSE  the text says no in its own words (a failed "where", an "unless" that is met, a flat negative, "other than");
--   NOTHING     not covered: the text is silent, points outside the sources, or limits a grant in its own wording (a named
--               complement, "with the consent of", "if" on a power) and the case falls outside the limit.
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into JUST FALSE. Every branch that answers JUST FALSE carries `-- FALSE-kind (...)` and every
-- branch that answers NOTHING carries `-- NOTHING-kind (...)`, each followed by the words of the text that license it.
-- Answers taken from another provision are INPUTS named for it (PLAN.md section 4), typed MAYBE BOOLEAN where that provision can say "not covered".

§ `Section 468 -- Superior court of criminal jurisdiction`

-- The court is a superior court of criminal jurisdiction as s 2 says (an input: the answer of `s 2 superior court of criminal jurisdiction`);
-- the offence is an indictable offence as the core's Class of offence says (PLAN.md R2).
-- src:8527 | Superior court of criminal jurisdiction
-- src:8528 | 468 Every superior court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction to try any indictable offence.
@ref s 468 (src:8527-8528)
GIVEN `s 2 superior court of criminal jurisdiction: the court is a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the offence is of this class` IS A `Class of offence`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 468 the superior court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction to try the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 2 superior court of criminal jurisdiction: the court is a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the court is a superior court of criminal jurisdiction is for s 2 and is not determined
        IF `s 2 superior court of criminal jurisdiction: the court is a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Every superior court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction"
        IF `the offence is of this class` EQUALS `an offence punishable on summary conviction` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to try any indictable offence"
        IF `the offence is of this class` EQUALS `an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not say whether an offence that is both is "any indictable offence" before the mode of proceeding is chosen (fork: hybrid)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 469 -- Court of criminal jurisdiction`

-- The charge is represented by the form it takes (the offence itself, accessory after the fact, attempt, conspiracy) and the offence it names, as the
-- core's Provision of the Code (PLAN.md R1: an offence named by section number is tested against `section`, as a decimal).
-- src:8529 | Court of criminal jurisdiction
-- src:8530 | 469 Every court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction to try an indictable offence other than
-- src:8531 | (a) an offence under any of the following sections:
-- src:8532 | (i) section 47 (treason),
-- src:8533 | (ii) [Repealed, 2018, c. 29, s. 61]
-- src:8534 | (iii) section 51 (intimidating Parliament or a legislature),
-- src:8535 | (iv) section 53 (inciting to mutiny),
-- src:8536 | (v) section 61 (seditious offences),
-- src:8537 | (vi) section 74 (piracy),
-- src:8538 | (vii) section 75 (piratical acts), or
-- src:8539 | (viii) section 235 (murder);
-- src:8540 | Accessories
-- src:8541 | (b) the offence of being an accessory after the fact to high treason or treason or murder;
-- src:8542 | (c) an offence under section 119 (bribery) by the holder of a judicial office;
-- src:8543 | Crimes against humanity
-- src:8544 | (c.1) an offence under any of sections 4 to 7 of the Crimes Against Humanity and War Crimes Act;
-- src:8545 | Attempts
-- src:8546 | (d) the offence of attempting to commit any offence mentioned in subparagraphs (a)(i) to (vii); or
-- src:8547 | Conspiracy
-- src:8548 | (e) the offence of conspiring to commit any offence mentioned in paragraph (a).
@ref s 469 (src:8529-8548)
DECLARE `s 469 form of the charge` IS ONE OF
    `s 469 the charge is of the offence itself`
    `s 469 the charge is of being an accessory after the fact to the offence named`
    `s 469 the charge is of attempting to commit the offence named`
    `s 469 the charge is of conspiring to commit the offence named`
    `s 469 the charge is of another form (for example counselling)`

@ref s 469 (src:8529-8548)
DECLARE `s 469 facts of the charge` HAS
    `the offence named`   IS A `Provision of the Code`   -- the offence charged (or the offence attempted, conspired about or accessory to); only `section` is read
    `the form of the charge`   IS A `s 469 form of the charge`
    `the offence is under any of sections 4 to 7 of the Crimes Against Humanity and War Crimes Act`   IS A BOOLEAN   -- (c.1): a section of another Act, so a fact; when TRUE `the offence named` is not read
    `the accused is the holder of a judicial office`   IS A BOOLEAN   -- (c)
    `the offence of which the accused is alleged to be an accessory after the fact is high treason, treason or murder`   IS A BOOLEAN   -- (b): the text names these offences, not section numbers (fork: names-not-numbers)

-- The offence is "listed in section 469" (the phrase s 473(1), 478(3) and 479 use, and the seam PLAN.md section 6 names for ss 498, 515, 522, 523, 536, 536.1, 554).
@ref s 469 (src:8529-8548)
GIVEN facts IS A `s 469 facts of the charge`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 469 the offence charged is one listed in section 469` MEANS
    IF `the charge is within one of paragraphs (a) to (e)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "has jurisdiction to try an indictable offence other than" -- the charge is in none of (a) to (e) (fork: closed-list)
    WHERE
        `the section named` MEANS facts's `the offence named`'s `section`
        `named in subparagraphs (a)(i) to (vii)` MEANS
                `the section named` EQUALS 47
            OR  `the section named` EQUALS 51
            OR  `the section named` EQUALS 53
            OR  `the section named` EQUALS 61
            OR  `the section named` EQUALS 74
            OR  `the section named` EQUALS 75
        `named in paragraph (a)` MEANS `named in subparagraphs (a)(i) to (vii)` OR `the section named` EQUALS 235
        `the charge is within one of paragraphs (a) to (e)` MEANS
            BRANCH
                IF facts's `the offence is under any of sections 4 to 7 of the Crimes Against Humanity and War Crimes Act` THEN TRUE   -- (c.1)
                IF facts's `the form of the charge` EQUALS `s 469 the charge is of the offence itself`
                    THEN `named in paragraph (a)` OR (`the section named` EQUALS 119 AND facts's `the accused is the holder of a judicial office`)   -- (a) and (c)
                IF facts's `the form of the charge` EQUALS `s 469 the charge is of being an accessory after the fact to the offence named`
                    THEN facts's `the offence of which the accused is alleged to be an accessory after the fact is high treason, treason or murder`   -- (b)
                IF facts's `the form of the charge` EQUALS `s 469 the charge is of attempting to commit the offence named`
                    THEN `named in subparagraphs (a)(i) to (vii)`   -- (d): "(a)(i) to (vii)" stops short of (viii), section 235: an attempt to commit murder is not listed
                IF facts's `the form of the charge` EQUALS `s 469 the charge is of conspiring to commit the offence named`
                    THEN `named in paragraph (a)`   -- (e): "paragraph (a)" takes in (viii), section 235
                OTHERWISE FALSE

-- The court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction to try the offence. A grant to "every court of criminal jurisdiction" for "an indictable offence other than" the listed ones.
-- Inputs: the court (the answer of s 2), the class of offence, and whether the offence is listed (the function above).
@ref s 469 (src:8529-8548)
GIVEN `s 2 court of criminal jurisdiction: the court is a court of criminal jurisdiction` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the offence is of this class` IS A `Class of offence`
      `the offence charged is one listed in section 469, by s 469` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 469 the court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction to try the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 2 court of criminal jurisdiction: the court is a court of criminal jurisdiction` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the court is a court of criminal jurisdiction is for s 2 and is not determined
        IF `s 2 court of criminal jurisdiction: the court is a court of criminal jurisdiction` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Every court of criminal jurisdiction has jurisdiction"
        IF `the offence is of this class` EQUALS `an offence punishable on summary conviction` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to try an indictable offence"
        IF `the offence is of this class` EQUALS `an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not say whether an offence that is both is "an indictable offence" before the mode of proceeding is chosen (fork: hybrid)
        IF `the offence charged is one listed in section 469, by s 469` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the offence is one of those in paragraphs (a) to (e) is not determined
        IF `the offence charged is one listed in section 469, by s 469` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "has jurisdiction to try an indictable offence other than" (fork: other-than)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- (a)(ii) is shown repealed: "(ii) [Repealed, 2018, c. 29, s. 61]". Carried as a quotation.
-- src:8533 | (ii) [Repealed, 2018, c. 29, s. 61]
@ref s 469(a)(ii), repealed (src:8533)
`s 469(a)(ii) as the text shows it` MEANS "(ii) [Repealed, 2018, c. 29, s. 61]"

§ `Section 470 -- Jurisdiction over person`

-- "That has power to try an indictable offence" is the answer of s 468 (a superior court) or of s 469 (a court of criminal jurisdiction): an input. "Subject to this Act" is taken as an input too:
-- whether another provision of this Act takes the competence away (its own answer is outside this section).
-- src:8549 | Jurisdiction over person
-- src:8550 | 470 Subject to this Act, every superior court of criminal jurisdiction and every court of criminal jurisdiction that has power to try an indictable offence is competent to try an accused for that offence
-- src:8551 | (a) if the accused is found, is arrested or is in custody within the territorial jurisdiction of the court; or
-- src:8552 | (b) if the accused has been ordered to be tried by
-- src:8553 | (i) that court, or
-- src:8554 | (ii) any other court, the jurisdiction of which has by lawful authority been transferred to that court.
@ref s 470 (src:8549-8554)
GIVEN `the court has power to try the indictable offence, by s 468 or s 469` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `another provision of this Act limits the court's competence to try the accused, by the provisions that limit s 470` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the accused is found within the territorial jurisdiction of the court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accused is arrested within the territorial jurisdiction of the court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accused is in custody within the territorial jurisdiction of the court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accused has been ordered to be tried by that court` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the accused has been ordered to be tried by another court the jurisdiction of which has by lawful authority been transferred to that court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 470 the court is competent to try the accused for the offence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the court has power to try the indictable offence, by s 468 or s 469` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the court has power to try the offence is for s 468 and s 469 and is not determined
        IF `the court has power to try the indictable offence, by s 468 or s 469` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "that has power to try an indictable offence"
        IF NOT (`another provision of this Act limits the court's competence to try the accused, by the provisions that limit s 470` EQUALS JUST FALSE) THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Subject to this Act" hands the question to the provisions that limit s 470 (fork: subject-to)
        IF    `the accused is found within the territorial jurisdiction of the court`
           OR `the accused is arrested within the territorial jurisdiction of the court`
           OR `the accused is in custody within the territorial jurisdiction of the court`
           THEN JUST TRUE   -- (a)
        IF    `the accused has been ordered to be tried by that court`
           OR `the accused has been ordered to be tried by another court the jurisdiction of which has by lawful authority been transferred to that court`
           THEN JUST TRUE   -- (b)(i) and (ii)
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "is competent to try an accused for that offence (a) if the accused is found, is arrested or is in custody within the territorial jurisdiction of the court" -- neither (a) nor (b) holds: a grant named for competence, its "if" limbs unmet (fork: if-on-power)

§ `Section 471 -- Trial by jury compulsory`

-- "Otherwise expressly provided by law" is the answer of the provisions that give a trial without a jury (s 473 in this range; ss 536 to 556 and others elsewhere): an input.
-- src:8555 | Trial by jury compulsory
-- src:8556 | 471 Except where otherwise expressly provided by law, every accused who is charged with an indictable offence shall be tried by a court composed of a judge and jury.
@ref s 471 (src:8555-8556)
GIVEN `the offence is of this class` IS A `Class of offence`
      `the law otherwise expressly provides, by the provisions that give a trial without a jury` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 471 the accused shall be tried by a court composed of a judge and jury` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the offence is of this class` EQUALS `an offence punishable on summary conviction` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "every accused who is charged with an indictable offence"
        IF `the offence is of this class` EQUALS `an offence that the section makes both indictable and punishable on summary conviction` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not say whether an offence that is both is "an indictable offence" before the mode of proceeding is chosen (fork: hybrid)
        IF `the law otherwise expressly provides, by the provisions that give a trial without a jury` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Except where otherwise expressly provided by law" (an exception that is met)
        IF `the law otherwise expressly provides, by the provisions that give a trial without a jury` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether another provision expressly provides otherwise is not determined
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 472 -- [Repealed]`

-- 472 [Repealed, R.S., 1985, c. 27 (1st Supp.), s. 63]. No operative text: carried as a quotation.
-- src:8557 | 472 [Repealed, R.S., 1985, c. 27 (1st Supp.), s. 63]
@ref s 472, repealed (src:8557)
`s 472 as the text shows it` MEANS "472 [Repealed, R.S., 1985, c. 27 (1st Supp.), s. 63]"

§ `Section 473 -- Trial without jury`

-- "Notwithstanding anything in this Act" overrides the rest of the Act, so no input stands for it (s 471 reads the answer of this subsection as its exception).
-- src:8558 | Trial without jury
-- src:8559 | 473 (1) Notwithstanding anything in this Act, an accused charged with an offence listed in section 469 may, with the consent of the accused and the Attorney General, be tried without a jury by a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction.
@ref s 473(1) (src:8558-8559)
GIVEN `the offence charged is one listed in section 469, by s 469` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the accused consents` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Attorney General consents` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 2 superior court of criminal jurisdiction: the trial is by a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 473(1) the accused may be tried without a jury by a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the offence charged is one listed in section 469, by s 469` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the offence is listed in section 469 is not determined
        IF `the offence charged is one listed in section 469, by s 469` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "an accused charged with an offence listed in section 469 may"
        IF NOT `the accused consents` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "with the consent of the accused and the Attorney General"
        IF NOT `the Attorney General consents` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "with the consent of the accused and the Attorney General"
        IF NOT (`s 2 superior court of criminal jurisdiction: the trial is by a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "be tried without a jury by a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:8560 | Joinder of other offences
-- src:8561 | (1.1) Where the consent of the accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), the judge of the superior court of criminal jurisdiction may order that any offence be tried by that judge in conjunction with the offence listed in section 469.
@ref s 473(1.1) (src:8560-8561)
GIVEN `the consent of the accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), by s 473(1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 473(1.1) the judge of the superior court of criminal jurisdiction may order that any offence be tried by that judge in conjunction with the offence listed in section 469` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the consent of the accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), by s 473(1)` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the consent is given in accordance with subsection (1) is for s 473(1) and is not determined
        IF `the consent of the accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), by s 473(1)` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the consent of the accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1)" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- The bar is titled as the bar: JUST TRUE means the withdrawal is barred. "Shall not be withdrawn unless both ... agree" is a flat negative with an "unless": when both agree the bar does not apply (JUST FALSE).
-- The text names no party who shall not withdraw: no actor is invented, so the rule is a decision function, not a regulative rule (fork: bare-must).
-- src:8562 | Withdrawal of consent
-- src:8563 | (2) Notwithstanding anything in this Act, where the consent of an accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), that consent shall not be withdrawn unless both the accused and the Attorney General agree to the withdrawal.
@ref s 473(2) (src:8562-8563)
GIVEN `the consent of an accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), by s 473(1)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `both the accused and the Attorney General agree to the withdrawal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 473(2) the withdrawal of the consent is barred` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the consent of an accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), by s 473(1)` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the consent is given in accordance with subsection (1) is for s 473(1) and is not determined
        IF `the consent of an accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1), by s 473(1)` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where the consent of an accused and the Attorney General is given in accordance with subsection (1)" (a failed "where")
        IF `both the accused and the Attorney General agree to the withdrawal` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless both the accused and the Attorney General agree to the withdrawal"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 474 -- Adjournment when no jury summoned`

-- The clerk is as s 2 "clerk of the court" says (an input). Official powers, encoded for what they mean to the accused: the court that is to try them may be put off by the clerk.
-- src:8564 | Adjournment when no jury summoned
-- src:8565 | 474 (1) Where the competent authority has determined that a panel of jurors is not to be summoned for a term or sittings of the court for the trial of criminal cases in any territorial division, the clerk of the court may, on the day of the opening of the term or sittings, if a judge is not present to preside over the court, adjourn the court and the business of the court to a subsequent day.
@ref s 474(1) (src:8564-8565)
GIVEN `s 2 clerk of the court: the person is the clerk of the court` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the competent authority has determined that a panel of jurors is not to be summoned for the term or sittings of the court for the trial of criminal cases in the territorial division` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is the day of the opening of the term or sittings` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a judge is present to preside over the court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 474(1) the clerk of the court may adjourn the court and the business of the court to a subsequent day` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`s 2 clerk of the court: the person is the clerk of the court` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "the clerk of the court may"
        IF NOT `the competent authority has determined that a panel of jurors is not to be summoned for the term or sittings of the court for the trial of criminal cases in the territorial division` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the competent authority has determined that a panel of jurors is not to be summoned" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)
        IF NOT `it is the day of the opening of the term or sittings` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "on the day of the opening of the term or sittings" (a period the text fixes in its own words)
        IF `a judge is present to preside over the court` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if a judge is not present to preside over the court" (an "if" on a power: fork: if-on-power)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:8566 | Adjournment on instructions of judge
-- src:8567 | (2) A clerk of the court for the trial of criminal cases in any territorial division may, at any time, on the instructions of the presiding judge or another judge of the court, adjourn the court and the business of the court to a subsequent day.
@ref s 474(2) (src:8566-8567)
GIVEN `s 2 clerk of the court: the person is a clerk of the court for the trial of criminal cases in the territorial division` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the clerk acts on the instructions of the presiding judge or another judge of the court` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 474(2) the clerk of the court may adjourn the court and the business of the court to a subsequent day on the instructions of a judge` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`s 2 clerk of the court: the person is a clerk of the court for the trial of criminal cases in the territorial division` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A clerk of the court for the trial of criminal cases in any territorial division may"
        IF NOT `the clerk acts on the instructions of the presiding judge or another judge of the court` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "on the instructions of the presiding judge or another judge of the court"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 475 -- Accused absconding during trial`

-- Whether the accused is charged jointly with another changes nothing ("whether or not"), so no input stands for it. "Absconds" is an open-textured fact, taken as a plain input.
-- src:8568 | Accused absconding during trial
-- src:8569 | 475 (1) Notwithstanding any other provision of this Act, where an accused, whether or not he is charged jointly with another, absconds during the course of his trial,
-- src:8570 | (a) he shall be deemed to have waived his right to be present at his trial, and
-- src:8571 | (b) the court may
-- src:8572 | (i) continue the trial and proceed to a judgment or verdict and, if it finds the accused guilty, impose a sentence on him in his absence, or
-- src:8573 | (ii) if a warrant in Form 7 is issued for the arrest of the accused, adjourn the trial to await his appearance,
-- src:8574 | but where the trial is adjourned pursuant to subparagraph (b)(ii), the court may, at any time, continue the trial if it is satisfied that it is no longer in the interests of justice to await the appearance of the accused.
@ref s 475(1) (src:8568-8574)
GIVEN `the accused absconds during the course of the trial` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(1)(a) the accused is deemed to have waived the right to be present at the trial` MEANS
    IF `the accused absconds during the course of the trial`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where an accused, whether or not he is charged jointly with another, absconds during the course of his trial" (a failed "where")

@ref s 475(1)(b)(i) (src:8571-8572)
GIVEN `the accused absconds during the course of the trial` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(1)(b)(i) the court may continue the trial and proceed to a judgment or verdict` MEANS
    IF `the accused absconds during the course of the trial`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where an accused, whether or not he is charged jointly with another, absconds during the course of his trial" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)

@ref s 475(1)(b)(i) (src:8571-8572)
GIVEN `the accused absconds during the course of the trial` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court finds the accused guilty` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(1)(b)(i) the court may impose a sentence on the accused in the accused's absence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the accused absconds during the course of the trial` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where an accused, whether or not he is charged jointly with another, absconds during the course of his trial" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)
        IF NOT `the court finds the accused guilty` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if it finds the accused guilty, impose a sentence on him in his absence" (an "if" on a power: fork: if-on-power)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 475(1)(b)(ii) (src:8571-8573)
GIVEN `the accused absconds during the course of the trial` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a warrant in Form 7 is issued for the arrest of the accused` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(1)(b)(ii) the court may adjourn the trial to await the accused's appearance` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the accused absconds during the course of the trial` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where an accused, whether or not he is charged jointly with another, absconds during the course of his trial" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)
        IF NOT `a warrant in Form 7 is issued for the arrest of the accused` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if a warrant in Form 7 is issued for the arrest of the accused" (an "if" on a power: fork: if-on-power)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 475(1), the proviso (src:8574)
GIVEN `the trial is adjourned pursuant to subparagraph (b)(ii)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that it is no longer in the interests of justice to await the appearance of the accused` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(1) the court may, at any time, continue a trial adjourned pursuant to subparagraph (b)(ii)` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the trial is adjourned pursuant to subparagraph (b)(ii)` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but where the trial is adjourned pursuant to subparagraph (b)(ii)" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)
        IF NOT `the court is satisfied that it is no longer in the interests of justice to await the appearance of the accused` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if it is satisfied that it is no longer in the interests of justice to await the appearance of the accused" (an "if" on a power: fork: if-on-power)
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:8575 | Adverse inference
-- src:8576 | (2) Where a court continues a trial pursuant to subsection (1), it may draw an inference adverse to the accused from the fact that he has absconded.
@ref s 475(2) (src:8575-8576)
GIVEN `the court continues the trial pursuant to subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(2) the court may draw an inference adverse to the accused from the fact that the accused has absconded` MEANS
    IF `the court continues the trial pursuant to subsection (1)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a court continues a trial pursuant to subsection (1)" (a failed "where" on a power: fork: where-on-power)

-- Titled as the bar the text states: JUST TRUE means the accused is not entitled to have the proceedings re-opened (fork: entitled; fork: unless-met).
-- src:8577 | Accused not entitled to re-opening
-- src:8578 | (3) Where an accused reappears at his trial that is continuing pursuant to subsection (1), he is not entitled to have any part of the proceedings that was conducted in his absence re-opened unless the court is satisfied that because of exceptional circumstances it is in the interests of justice to re-open the proceedings.
@ref s 475(3) (src:8577-8578)
GIVEN `the accused reappears at the trial that is continuing pursuant to subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court is satisfied that because of exceptional circumstances it is in the interests of justice to re-open the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(3) the accused is not entitled to have any part of the proceedings conducted in the accused's absence re-opened` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the accused reappears at the trial that is continuing pursuant to subsection (1)` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an accused reappears at his trial that is continuing pursuant to subsection (1)" (a failed "where")
        IF `the court is satisfied that because of exceptional circumstances it is in the interests of justice to re-open the proceedings` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the court is satisfied that because of exceptional circumstances it is in the interests of justice to re-open the proceedings"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- Titled as the question the subsection answers: does it preserve counsel's authority (JUST TRUE), or does it say nothing for these facts (JUST FALSE: a failed "where").
-- src:8579 | Counsel for accused may continue to act
-- src:8580 | (4) Where an accused has absconded during the course of his trial and the court continues the trial, counsel for the accused is not thereby deprived of any authority he may have to continue to act for the accused in the proceedings.
@ref s 475(4) (src:8579-8580)
GIVEN `the accused has absconded during the course of the trial` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court continues the trial` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 475(4) the subsection preserves any authority counsel for the accused has to continue to act for the accused in the proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the accused has absconded during the course of the trial` AND `the court continues the trial`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where an accused has absconded during the course of his trial and the court continues the trial" (a failed "where")
